# Line endings, pinned the same way the source repo pins them.
#
# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true in the SYSTEM config, so without this
# file every checkout rewrites these files to CRLF and git prints "LF will be
# replaced by CRLF" for each one. .gitattributes overrides autocrlf, which makes
# it the only reliable place to say this.
#
# It matters more here than it looks: the staged tree is compared byte-for-byte
# against its source by the drift check, so a CRLF rewrite in the working copy
# would report drift and a line-endings warning on every run, forever.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The script is run on Windows, where CRLF is native -- but PowerShell reads LF
# perfectly well, and matching the source repo keeps one rule instead of two.
*.ps1 text eol=lf
